│ Task: OWASP A08 Software or Data Integrity Failures: unsafe deserialization,           │
│ unverified updates and assets, and trust placed in client-supplied or third-party      │
│ data.                                                                                  │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Find integrity assumptions                                                      │
│ Objective: Locate where the application trusts data it should verify.                  │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Identify endpoints accepting serialized objects, type hints, polymorphic payloads │
│ or object graphs, from recovered source where available.                               │
│   2. Look for client-supplied values used in security decisions, pricing, identity or  │
│ state transitions.                                                                     │
│   3. Check whether third-party assets are loaded without integrity verification, and   │
│ whether update or plugin mechanisms verify signatures.                                 │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Test carefully and honestly                                                     │
│ Objective: Deserialization claims are where reports most often overstate.              │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. If a deserializer accepts a type discriminator, first read the recovered source   │
│ to learn the exact discriminator and the types actually permitted. Guessing a          │
│ discriminator produces an 'unknown type' error that proves nothing.                    │
│   2. A type that merely exists is not a gadget: a gadget chain must INVOKE something.  │
│ If you cannot demonstrate invocation, the finding is UNVERIFIED and must be reported   │
│ that way.                                                                              │
│   3. Presence of a vulnerable-looking component or version is not an integrity         │
│ finding. Do not cite an advisory identifier unless you have executed it against this   │
│ target and observed the effect.                                                        │
│   4. Test tampering with client-supplied values that influence server-side decisions,  │
│ and show the changed outcome.                                                          │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Verify before reporting                                                         │
│ Objective: Apply the same evidence standard to every finding, so results are           │
│ comparable across categories.                                                          │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. PROOF DISCIPLINE - label every finding DEMONSTRATED (you executed it and the      │
│ response proves it, quote that response), INFERRED (follows from evidence but was not  │
│ executed), or UNVERIFIED (suspected from a version, component or code pattern only).   │
│ Identifying software is never a demonstration; never write 'confirmed' without having  │
│ executed something.                                                                    │
│   2. RUN THE CONTROL TEST. Before claiming any access or bypass, repeat the request    │
│ WITHOUT the credential, token, header or material you believe is responsible. If the   │
│ result is the same, you have proven nothing - the resource was already reachable.      │
│ State the control result alongside the finding. This single omission is the most       │
│ common source of false critical findings.                                              │
│   3. BUDGET DISCIPLINE - rank leads by evidence strength (material in hand > observed  │
│ behaviour > suspicion from a version string) and give each a step budget. STOP RULE:   │
│ about 8-10 consecutive steps on one lead with no new evidence means move on, recording │
│ what you tried.                                                                        │
│   4. Record findings to notes as you go, with the endpoint, the request, the response  │
│ and the control result, so later phases and the report can use them.                   │
│   5. Stay non-destructive: prefer read-only proof, do not modify or delete data, do    │
│ not lock accounts, and stop once the point is demonstrated.                            │
│                                                                                        │
│                                                                                        │
